Each RFT and RPT employee receives one floating holiday per calendar year. A floating holiday:
- may be used for a cultural, religious, civic, or personal observance;
- should normally be requested at least five business days in advance;
- is deducted based on the employee's scheduled hours, up to eight hours;
- requires manager approval for operational coverage;
- does not carry over after December 31; and
- is not paid out at separation unless required by law.
A new employee hired on or before September 30 receives the floating holiday for that year. A later hire does not receive it unless the offer letter says otherwise.

A role is eligible only when its duties can be performed without material loss of customer service, collaboration, supervision, security, equipment access, or operational continuity. The manager and People Operations consider:
- role duties and need for physical presence;
- access to Confidential or Restricted data;
- performance, reliability, and ability to work independently;
- the proposed workspace, connectivity, and time-zone overlap;
- team coverage and customer commitments; and
- jurisdiction, payroll, tax, immigration, licensing, or client restrictions.
A recurring Hybrid or Remote arrangement normally begins after the first 60 calendar days of employment unless the employee was hired into that designation, an accommodation applies, or People Operations approves an earlier start.

The employee must normally:
- be RFT or RPT and have completed 90 calendar days of service;
- be meeting performance and conduct expectations;
- have a role that can be performed from the proposed location;
- use company-managed equipment and approved connectivity;
- maintain required time-zone overlap and customer coverage;
- have no client, licensing, export-control, or data-residency restriction that prevents the arrangement; and
- demonstrate a lawful basis to be present and work in the destination.
An employee is responsible for truthful travel and authorization information. Northstar does not provide personal immigration or tax advice.

A complete request must include:
1. employee ID, role, manager, and Registered Home Office;
2. destination city, region, and country;
3. arrival and departure dates and expected business days worked;
4. reason for the request and whether the travel is personal or business;
5. work schedule and time-zone overlap;
6. systems, data classifications, customers, and regulated activities involved;
7. proposed workspace, network, equipment, and emergency contact method;
8. immigration or work-authorization basis known to the employee; and
9. any prior out-of-jurisdiction days in the rolling 12-month period.
An incomplete request may be returned for clarification rather than denied.

Approvers assess, as applicable:
- immigration and permission to work;
- income-tax withholding, payroll registration, social contributions, and corporate permanent-establishment risk;
- employment standards, licensing, sanctions, export controls, and local registration;
- health, travel, workers' compensation, and insurance coverage;
- cybersecurity, data residency, government access, and device inspection risk;
- customer contracts and Restricted-data access;
- time-zone coverage, productivity, and emergency support; and
- the cumulative duration and pattern of prior approvals.
No single approver may waive another function's legal or security requirement.

**POL-SEC-001** applies at all times. International approvals normally require:
- company-managed device, current patches, disk encryption, MFA, and company VPN;
- no use of shared or public computers;
- no printing or local storage of Restricted information;
- private workspace and secure device storage;
- immediate reporting of device loss, border inspection, credential concerns, or suspected compromise; and
- explicit Security approval before accessing Restricted data outside the payroll country.
Security may issue a travel device, restrict applications, disable downloads, or require password changes after return.

An employee must not, without specific written authority:
- sign contracts or create a local business presence;
- hire or manage local workers outside an approved structure;
- represent that Northstar has an office in the destination;
- change the payroll address or employment location based only on temporary approval;
- perform regulated work requiring a local license; or
- continue working after approval expires.

A request may be:
- **Approved** for the stated dates and conditions;
- **Approved with conditions**, such as reduced duration, limited systems, specific hours, or a travel device;
- **Returned for clarification**;
- **Escalated** for Legal, Security, tax, client, or executive review; or
- **Not approved** when risks cannot be reasonably controlled.
The decision and conditions are recorded in the HR case system. A material change in destination, dates, duties, data, or travel status requires a new review.

Confidential or Restricted information may be accessed only from a company-managed device or a specifically approved managed device. Personal computers may not download, synchronize, or store Northstar information. Limited browser access to low-risk Internal information may be allowed only through approved services with MFA and no local download.
Employees must:
- use unique company credentials and MFA;
- never share passwords, tokens, badges, or devices;
- install only approved software;
- allow required patching, encryption, endpoint protection, and device management;
- lock the screen when unattended; and
- use the approved password manager for company secrets.

Examples include:
- personal travel, companion costs, sightseeing, and leisure extensions;
- upgrades or luxury items without required approval;
- commuting, ordinary clothing, personal grooming, and personal household costs;
- fines, penalties, towing caused by a violation, and personal legal costs;
- minibar, in-room entertainment, and personal subscriptions;
- unapproved equipment or software;
- duplicate claims, loyalty-point value, or costs already paid by another party; and
- expenses that violate law, contract, or company policy.

Onboarding must:
- collect only necessary information and protect it appropriately;
- provide access based on least privilege and verified role need;
- make policy, conduct, security, and reporting expectations clear;
- provide the equipment and support required for the approved work designation;
- assign accountable owners and due dates; and
- avoid granting access before required approvals, identity checks, or agreements are complete.

The following should be completed or scheduled before the start date:
1. signed offer and required agreements;
2. identity and right-to-work process, where applicable;
3. tax, payroll, emergency contact, and banking information through approved secure systems;
4. employment category, Registered Home Office, manager, cost centre, and work designation;
5. benefits eligibility notice date;
6. background or credential checks authorized for the role;
7. equipment and shipping request under **POL-EQP-001**;
8. account and role-based access requests;
9. first-week schedule, orientation invitations, and onboarding buddy; and
10. accommodation or accessibility needs handled confidentially by People Operations.
Sensitive information must not be collected through ordinary email when a secure form is available.

On day one, the employee should receive:
- identity and account activation support;
- manager welcome and role overview;
- approved work schedule and work-location expectations;
- access to policies, reporting channels, and the employee portal;
- equipment inventory and acceptable-use acknowledgement;
- payroll and timekeeping instructions; and
- a first-week plan.
A manager must not ask a new employee to share credentials or use another employee's account while access is pending.

A concern may be raised through any appropriate channel:
• a manager or next-level manager;
• People Operations through the case portal or peopleops@northstar.example;
• Ethics through ethics@northstar.example;
• Information Security for security incidents; or
• local emergency services for immediate danger.
A reporter may request confidentiality or use an available anonymous channel. Confidentiality cannot be guaranteed
because Northstar may need to investigate, protect people, or comply with law. Information will be limited to those
with a legitimate need.

Cases may enter through:
• the HR case portal;
• peopleops@northstar.example;
• ethics@northstar.example;
• a manager or next-level manager;
• Security or IT incident escalation;
• the benefits or payroll support process; or
• an approved anonymous reporting channel.
A case received in another channel should be transferred to the appropriate controlled record when necessary.
Sensitive details should not be copied into broad email lists or team chat.

A case should capture only what is necessary:
1. reporter name and employee ID, or anonymous status;
2. person or group affected;
3. issue category and concise description;
4. dates, location, and whether the issue is ongoing;
5. immediate safety, retaliation, access, pay, or medical urgency;
6. relevant documents or witnesses known to the reporter;
7. preferred safe contact method; and
8. requested help or outcome, recognizing that Northstar decides the process.
A reporter should not upload unnecessary medical records, identity documents, passwords, or unrelated personal
material.

Escalate immediately when a case involves:
• immediate danger, violence, threat, or severe safety event - to Security and executive response;
• suspected criminal conduct, bribery, material fraud, or legal hold - to Ethics/Legal and Finance as appropriate;
• significant data exposure or credential compromise - to Information Security;
• harassment, discrimination, retaliation, or senior-leader allegation - to Employee Relations/Ethics with conflict
review;
• urgent medical accommodation or protected leave - to the restricted People Operations team; or
• repeated service failure, missed critical target, or unresolved owner conflict - to the People Operations leader.
A manager receiving a report covered by POL-CON-001 must escalate within one business day and sooner when
urgent.
